HELION_PAPERINFO_PROMPT = """Below is a single, dense reference you can feed to a code-generating model so it can actually write correct and fast Helion kernels. It focuses on concrete API surface and syntax, the control structures that become device code, legal host/device boundaries, the memory primitives you must use to move data, and idiomatic patterns—matmul, elementwise, reductions, scans, and autograd wrappers—while intentionally avoiding discussion of autotuners or search strategies and instead spelling out exactly what to type and where.

A Helion kernel is a Python function decorated with `@helion.kernel(...)` that returns one or more tensors; everything written outside Helion device loops runs on the host exactly like PyTorch (compute or check shapes, resolve dtypes and devices, and allocate outputs with `torch.empty([...], dtype=..., device=...)`), and everything written inside Helion device loops—loops created with `hl.tile(...)` or `hl.grid(...)`—is compiled into exactly one Triton GPU kernel per Helion function. You always import `torch, helion, helion.language as hl`, then define a function whose parameters are standard PyTorch tensors (optionally accompanied by simple non-tensor scalars), and you must finish by returning a tensor or tuple of tensors. Kernel arguments carry sizes and strides implicitly so you index with tile objects rather than doing manual pointer arithmetic, and masking for edge tiles is implicit and erased when not needed. Outer device loops map to the launch grid while inner device loops run sequentially within a GPU program instance, and indexing, strides, grid size, PID calculation, and masking are generated automatically from your tile structure.

Declare kernels with `@helion.kernel()` or with `@helion.kernel(static_shapes=True)` when you want shape specialization for performance. The canonical structure is: assert and normalize shapes and dtypes on the host; allocate every output tensor before any device loop begins; open one or more device loops using `hl.tile(...)` or `hl.grid(...)`; perform math using PyTorch operators that TorchInductor can lower or using Helion’s explicit memory operators; store results back through tile indexing; return the output object. Kernel signatures must be explicit and positional—do not use `*args`, `**kwargs`, or keyword-only parameters in a kernel signature—and anything you write between top-level device loops is disallowed; globals and closures referenced inside the kernel body are automatically lifted into kernel arguments. All PyTorch ops invoked in device code must be traceable with `make_fx`, which includes common pointwise transforms, reductions, softmax, views, and matrix ops.

Iteration constructs come in two forms that model should use correctly and consistently. The tiled iterator `hl.tile(...)` subdivides an iteration space into tile objects; a single-dimension call produces one tile that represents a contiguous block of indices, and a multi-dimension call produces a tuple of tiles, one per dimension. These tiles are values you place directly in tensor subscripts, so a statement like `tensor[tm, tn]` implicitly performs a tile-shaped load or store from global memory. A tile object exposes metadata both as properties and as helper functions: `tile.index` gives an offset tensor for indexing within the tile; `tile.begin` and `tile.end` are scalar bounds; `tile.block_size` is the logical tile size; and `tile.id` is a logical identifier computed from begin and block size; equivalent free functions exist as `hl.tile_index(tile)`, `hl.tile_begin(tile)`, `hl.tile_end(tile)`, `hl.tile_block_size(tile)`, and `hl.tile_id(tile)`. The scalar iterator `hl.grid(...)` returns integer indices rather than tiles and is best used when you genuinely need scalar indexing instead of block-shaped vectorization; the two forms are interchangeable at the semantic level but produce different code shapes. For tight small loops that should be compile-time unrolled, use `hl.static_range(start, stop, step=1)` as a device-side range that maps to statically unrolled control flow.

Memory movement and update semantics are explicit and tile-aware. Tensor subscripting with tiles is the most concise form, where `val = a[tm]` is an implicit load and `out[tm] = val` is an implicit store, but you can use explicit site-annotated operations when you need more control or clarity. The explicit load `hl.load(tensor, [tile_or_indices, ...], extra_mask=None, eviction_policy="evict_first"|"evict_last"|"" )` reads a block from global memory and returns a device tensor shaped like the tile; `extra_mask` can restrict activity, and `eviction_policy` can be the empty string for no hint, or one of the two named strings to request early or late eviction. The explicit store `hl.store(tensor, [tile_or_indices, ...], value, extra_mask=None)` writes a device tensor into global memory at the given region and accepts an optional extra mask. When multiple threads must update the same address, use the atomic family with the same indexing rules: `hl.atomic_add`, `hl.atomic_and`, `hl.atomic_or`, `hl.atomic_xor`, `hl.atomic_xchg`, `hl.atomic_max`, `hl.atomic_min`, and `hl.atomic_cas`, each taking a target tensor, a list of tile or scalar indices, and the value to combine. For on-device diagnostics, `hl.device_print(...)` prints from device code. Synchronization across producer/consumer phases is available with `hl.signal(semaphore, [index], value)` and `hl.wait(semaphore, [index], signal=value)`, matching the common semaphore-style interface in GPU kernels.

Temporary device values and construction utilities ensure you keep intermediates in registers or fast local storage. Use `hl.zeros([tile_or_shape, ...], dtype=...)` to create zero-initialized device tensors whose shapes are inferred from tiles or provided explicitly, `hl.full([tile_or_shape, ...], value=..., dtype=...)` to allocate filled temporaries, and `hl.arange(...)` to create device-side integer ranges for indexing math. For small thread-local temporaries with stack/register semantics, construct a `StackTensor` via `hl.stacktensor_like(tensor)`; the class `helion.language.StackTensor` marks these for placement in local storage instead of global memory. In bandwidth- and stability-sensitive code, accumulate in higher precision by picking an accumulator dtype (for example `torch.float32` for fp16/bf16 inputs) when constructing temporaries and then cast back to the destination dtype immediately before storing, using `to(...)` on the accumulator tile just prior to a subscript store.

View and indexing utilities let you reshape and compose indices entirely on device. `hl.subscript(tensor, indices_or_tiles)` constructs an explicit subscript expression when you need to pass an indexed view through another operator rather than reading eagerly. `hl.split(tensor, dims_or_factors)` lets you split a dimension into multiple factors inside device code, and `hl.join(...)` fuses dimensions; these are device-side analogues of view-like transforms and keep layout work in the device region. Broadcasting semantics match PyTorch; you may rely on elementwise ops to broadcast when applied to tile-indexed tensors, or pre-broadcast on the host with `torch.broadcast_tensors(...)` or `torch.broadcast_to(...)` and then index the broadcasted shapes by tiles in the kernel.

Reductions and scans are first-class and lowered in device code. Standard expressions such as `x[tile].sum()`, `x[tile].max()`, or `torch.softmax(x[tile], dim=...)` are valid inside device loops and compile into proper reductions; for custom patterns, `hl.reduce(combine_fn, input_tensor, dim=None, other=0, keep_dims=False)` provides a generic reduction hook that accepts a combining function and describes axes and identity. Inclusive prefix operations are available as `hl.cumsum(x, dim)` and `hl.cumprod(x, dim)`, while generalized associative scans use `hl.associative_scan(combine_fn, input_tensor, dim)`. For dot-product-like work, call `hl.dot(a, b)` or rely on `torch.addmm` inside device loops; precision control is handled by the kernel settings and you should write the math expression, not the policy. For backends that need explicit low-level hooks, `hl.inline_triton(src: str, output_like=..., **format_args)` embeds a Triton snippet whose final expression is the return value and whose placeholders are filled via standard `str.format`, and `hl.inline_asm_elementwise(asm, inputs..., pack=..., output_like=...)` executes target-specific inline assembly in an elementwise, broadcast-aware fashion.

Compile-time constants and specialization give you predictable code shaping without leaving device context. Use `hl.constexpr(x)` to tag a value as a compile-time constant and to enable static folding of control flow and loop bounds, and accept `hl.constexpr` parameters in a kernel signature when you want the caller to supply constants that shape code. Use `hl.specialize(expr)` in device code when you want the compiler to commit to a branch based on something computable at compile time; avoid calling specialization helpers in host context and respect the restriction that specialization must not depend on runtime tensor data. When you want to expose knobs to your own wrappers or templates, register them explicitly: `hl.register_block_size(...)` produces an integer you can use as a block size or in tile range construction, `hl.register_tunable("name", candidates_or_bounds)` produces a value usable anywhere an integer or small enum is expected, and `hl.register_reduction_dim(size)` declares a reduction-dimension symbol for clarity. These registration calls are recognized at compile time and make your intent explicit; use them to keep numerical parameters and tile semantics spelled out in code rather than buried in ad-hoc arithmetic.

Configuration objects exist to define execution policy without discussing any search. When you pass `config=helion.Config(...)` to the decorator, you select a concrete execution strategy. The primary options a model should set or recognize are: `block_sizes` as a list of integers that define tile extents for each `hl.tile(...)` dimension; `loop_orders` as lists of permutations describing nested iteration orders; `flatten_loops` as booleans that determine whether multi-dimensional tiles are flattened; `indexing` as one of `"pointer"`, `"block_ptr"`, or `"tensor_descriptor"` to choose addressing mode; `pid_type` as `"flat"`, `"xyz"`, `"persistent_blocked"`, or `"persistent_interleaved"` to choose program-ID layout and persistent-kernel style; `num_warps` as the number of warps per block and `num_stages` as the pipeline depth; `l2_groupings` as integers that influence program-ID reordering for L2 locality; `reduction_loops` as integers or `None` to influence how reductions are structured; and the range-loop controls `range_unroll_factors`, `range_num_stages`, and `range_multi_buffers` that map to unrolling, pipelining, and accumulator multi-buffering for loops lowered through range constructs. You may also enable or disable static range emission through a setting in this family and pass load-site hints as `load_eviction_policies` alongside your code-level `eviction_policy` arguments when using `hl.load`. For development-time visibility, `@helion.kernel(print_output_code=True)` prints the generated Triton with `# src[file:line]` origin annotations so you can correlate Helion statements to backend code, and `static_shapes=True` requests shape specialization at compile time to bake exact sizes and strides into the generated kernel.

Idiomatic operation patterns are expressed as requirements rather than examples. For elementwise transforms, the model must allocate an output shaped like the input on the host, iterate over the full extent with a single `hl.tile(...)` or `hl.grid(...)` at device scope, read all inputs via tile subscripts, apply a PyTorch-lowerable elementwise expression, and write results back via tile subscripts; broadcasting is either implicit in the expression or performed on the host prior to the kernel call. For reductions, the model must open device loops over the output region at the top level, allocate an accumulator tile inside the device region with an appropriate high-precision dtype, iterate a device loop over the reduction dimension, combine the current slice with the accumulator using a mathematically correct and numerically stable operator, cast the accumulator to the destination dtype, and store it to the output tile; for matmul-shaped work, the two outer device tiles cover `m` and `n` while the inner device tile covers `k`, and the combining expression is a dot product or `addmm`. For fused epilogues, the model should apply bias, scaling, and activation directly to the accumulator tile immediately before storing to global memory to avoid additional kernel boundaries. For streaming long reductions with fixed register footprints, the model should drive the inner device loop with `hl.static_range(...)` or a range-lowered loop, use explicit `hl.load(...)` calls with optional eviction policy strings when appropriate, and keep the rolling accumulator in registers or a `StackTensor` scratch.

Backpropagation and PyTorch integration are standardized and must follow the framework’s rules. To produce trainable operations, define a subclass of `torch.autograd.Function` whose `forward` method validates shapes and dtypes, calls the Helion kernel function(s), and saves anything needed for gradients via `ctx.save_for_backward(...)` together with any scalar metadata on `ctx`; and whose `backward` method reconstructs the necessary transposes, broadcasts, and reductions using Helion kernels and returns a tuple of gradients aligned with the original inputs, using `None` for non-tensor arguments. A convenience wrapper function should call the `.apply(...)` interface and present a standard Pythonic API; the gradient computation should use the same tiling discipline described above and must ensure proper dtype promotion (for example, float32 accumulation for half-precision inputs) and final casting before stores.

Constraints and diagnostics are explicit, and the model must respect them to keep generated code valid. Device APIs from `helion.language` are legal only inside device loops; using them on the host triggers errors such as `helion.exc.NotInsideKernel` or `helion.exc.DeviceAPIOnHost`. Only `hl.tile(...)` and `hl.grid(...)` may appear in `for` headers to create device loops; calling them as expressions or placing host statements between top-level device loops triggers errors such as `LoopFunctionNotInFor`, `TopLevelStatementBetweenLoops`, `NestedGridLoop`, or `InvalidDeviceForLoop`. Indexing must match rank and use supported types or `RankMismatch` and `InvalidIndexingType` will be raised; assignments must target tensors with legal subscripts or you will encounter `RequiresTensorInAssignment`, `NonTensorSubscriptAssign`, or `DeviceTensorSubscriptAssignmentNotAllowed`. True loop-carried dependencies across device iterations are illegal (`LoopDependencyError`); host tensors may not be read directly from device code (`HostTensorDirectUsage`); and data-dependent output shapes are not supported inside device regions (`DataDependentOutputShapeNotSupported`). Kernels must have at least one tensor argument; starred arguments are not permitted; closures and global mutation are disallowed or restricted and trigger named exceptions. When you knowingly perform an unfused tensor operation on the host wrapper, you may silence specific compilation warnings by passing `ignore_warnings=[helion.exc.TensorOperationInWrapper]` or silence all Helion warnings with `ignore_warnings=[helion.exc.BaseWarning]` on the decorator for that kernel.

Putting this together, a code-generating model should reliably emit kernels that: import `torch, helion, helion.language as hl`; declare `@helion.kernel(static_shapes=True)` when shape specialization is desired; perform all checks and allocations before any device loop; open device loops with `hl.tile(...)` or `hl.grid(...)` to define the grid and any nested device loops; index tensors with tiles for coalesced reads and writes; use `hl.load`, `hl.store`, and the atomic family when explicit control or cross-thread updates are needed; build temporaries with `hl.zeros`, `hl.full`, `hl.arange`, and `StackTensor`; reshape and compose indices on device with `hl.subscript`, `hl.split`, and `hl.join`; express elementwise, reduction, scan, dot, and softmax math using PyTorch-lowerable ops or `hl.reduce`/`hl.associative_scan`/`hl.cumsum`/`hl.cumprod`/`hl.dot`; embed backend snippets with `hl.inline_triton` or target ISA with `hl.inline_asm_elementwise` when unavoidable; use `hl.constexpr` and `hl.specialize` for compile-time shaping; provide concrete execution policy via `helion.Config(...)` when needed, selecting among `block_sizes`, `loop_orders`, `flatten_loops`, `indexing`, `pid_type`, `num_warps`, `num_stages`, `l2_groupings`, `reduction_loops`, and the `range_*` controls; and integrate with PyTorch autograd by wrapping forward and backward in a `torch.autograd.Function` while honoring dtype promotion, broadcasting semantics, and gradient return ordering. This is the complete API-heavy surface necessary for an LLM to synthesize Helion kernels that stay within the legal host/device boundary, exercise the full GPU through correct tiling and memory access, and fuse epilogues to minimize extra launches—all without relying on any autotuning-specific behavior.
"""
